IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coroners-dates`

-- Coroners Act 1997 (Australian Capital Territory) -- the inquest into a death, from the seat of a member of the
-- deceased person's immediate family.
--
-- SEAT: a member of the immediate family of the person who died (dictionary: domestic partner, parent, step-parent,
-- grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian or ward; and, for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person, a
-- person with responsibility for or an interest in their welfare under their community's traditions), or that member's
-- representative. "Must there be an inquest? Can I see the body? Can I stop the post-mortem? Will there be a hearing,
-- and will I be told? Can I take part? Can I get the findings? The inquest has stopped because of a prosecution: when
-- can it go on? Can I ask for a hearing, or go to the Supreme Court?" Inquest findings on the manner and cause of death
-- are often the first public account a family has when it considers a claim for compensation.
--
-- The Act's provisions are addressed to coroners, the Chief Coroner, the Attorney-General and the Supreme Court. They
-- are encoded as what they mean for the family member: what they may ask for, what they must be given, and by when.
-- Every opinion, belief or satisfaction the Act leaves to an official is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: Republication No 51 (R51), effective 5 September 2026, ACT Legislation Register, retrieved 6 November 2026,
-- as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A1997-57.txt. No instrument brought in (see NOTES.md: the regulation
-- that s 68A(2) would prescribe and the Court Procedures Rules 2006 schedule 4 that s 98 applies are not deposited).
--
-- The witness's seat (including a person the coroner may criticise, and a holder of the deceased's records) is in
-- coroners-witness.l4; the seat of the owner or occupier of property (fire inquiries, search warrants, coronial
-- investigation scenes) is in coroners-owner.l4. The three seats share only coroners-dates.l4.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the family member's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage
-- table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.

§ `Coroners Act 1997 (ACT) -- a member of the deceased's immediate family`

§§ `Dictionary -- am I a member of the immediate family`

-- Dictionary: "member of the immediate family, of a deceased person the subject of an inquest, means-- (a) a person who
-- was the deceased person's domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, or guardian or
-- ward; and (b) if the deceased person was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person--a person who, in accordance
-- with the traditions and customs of the deceased person's Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander community, had the
-- responsibility for, or an interest in, the welfare of the deceased person."
-- "domestic partner" is the Legislation Act 2001's (s 169), borrowed: asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Relationship to the deceased` IS ONE OF
    `domestic partner`
    `parent`
    `step-parent`
    `grandparent`
    `child`
    `brother or sister`
    `guardian`
    `ward`
    `another relative, or a friend`

DECLARE `Immediate family answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I was the deceased's domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian or ward (dictionary, member of the immediate family, paragraph (a))`
    `yes: the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person and under the traditions and customs of their community I had the responsibility for, or an interest in, their welfare (dictionary, paragraph (b))`
    `no: the definition of member of the immediate family does not include me`

@export Am I a member of the immediate family of the person who died, for the Coroners Act?
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc How you were related to the person who died. One of: domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian, ward, another relative or a friend
      `the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person (descended from, identifying as, and accepted by the community as one)
      `under their community's traditions I had responsibility for or an interest in their welfare` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, under the traditions and customs of the deceased's Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander community, you had the responsibility for, or an interest in, their welfare
GIVETH AN `Immediate family answer`
DECIDE `Am I a member of the immediate family question` IS
    IF (NOT (`my relationship` EQUALS `another relative, or a friend`))
    THEN `yes: I was the deceased's domestic partner, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, brother or sister, guardian or ward (dictionary, member of the immediate family, paragraph (a))`
    ELSE IF     `the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person`
            AND `under their community's traditions I had responsibility for or an interest in their welfare`
         THEN `yes: the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person and under the traditions and customs of their community I had the responsibility for, or an interest in, their welfare (dictionary, paragraph (b))`
         ELSE `no: the definition of member of the immediate family does not include me`

§§ `ss 3BB, 3C, 3D -- was it a death in care or a death in custody`

-- s 3BB(1): "death in care means the death of a person-- (a) while being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to
-- an order, under-- (i) the Mental Health Act 2015; or (ii) the Crimes Act 1900, section 309 ...; or (b) because of a
-- fatal injury sustained in circumstances mentioned in subsection (a)."
-- s 3C(1): "death in custody means the death of a person (other than a death in care)-- (a) at a correction centre,
-- lockup or detention place; or (b) while performing work under a community service condition of a good behaviour order
-- ...; or (c) while subject to an accommodation order ... section 133Z ...; or (d) while subject to a transfer arrangement
-- under the Children and Young People Act 2008, division 5.2.1 ... or, while in custody under that Act, section 128 ...;
-- or (e) while at a licensed place under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994; or (f) while in, being
-- taken into, or after being taken into, the custody of a custodial officer; or (g) while escaping, or attempting to
-- escape, from the custody of a custodial officer, other than a carer under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection)
-- Act 1994." (2): "Also, death in custody includes death because of a fatal injury sustained in a place, or in
-- circumstances, mentioned in subsection (1)."
-- The fatal-injury limbs (s 3BB(1)(b), s 3C(2)) are folded into the input's wording: the place or circumstance is where
-- the person was when they died, or when they sustained the fatal injury.
DECLARE `Where the person was` IS ONE OF
    `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Mental Health Act 2015`
    `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Crimes Act 1900 section 309 (assessment whether emergency detention is required)`
    `at a correction centre, lockup or detention place`
    `performing community service work under a good behaviour order (Crimes (Sentencing) Act 2005)`
    `subject to an accommodation order for a young offender (Crimes (Sentencing) Act 2005 section 133Z)`
    `subject to an interstate transfer arrangement, or in transit custody, under the Children and Young People Act 2008`
    `at a licensed place under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994`
    `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody`
    `escaping, or attempting to escape, from someone's custody`
    `none of these`

-- s 3D: "custodial officer means any of the following: (a) a police officer; (b) the person in charge of a correctional
-- centre; (c) a corrections officer; (d) the chief psychiatrist; (e) a mental health officer; (f) the director-general
-- responsible for the Children and Young People Act 2008 or an authorised person under that Act; (g) the sheriff, a
-- deputy sheriff, or a person appointed to assist the sheriff, under the Supreme Court Act 1933; (h) a carer under the
-- Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994."
DECLARE `Whose custody` IS ONE OF
    `a police officer`
    `the person in charge of a correctional centre`
    `a corrections officer`
    `the chief psychiatrist`
    `a mental health officer`
    `the Children and Young People Act director-general or a person authorised under that Act`
    `the sheriff, a deputy sheriff, or a person appointed to assist the sheriff`
    `a carer under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994`
    `someone else (for example a security guard or a hospital)`
    `no one (the question does not arise)`

GIVEN `who` IS A `Whose custody`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a custodial officer` `who` IF
        (NOT (`who` EQUALS `someone else (for example a security guard or a hospital)`))
    AND (NOT (`who` EQUALS `no one (the question does not arise)`))

DECLARE `Care or custody` IS ONE OF
    `a death in care (s 3BB)`
    `a death in custody (s 3C)`
    `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`

GIVEN `where` IS A `Where the person was`, `who` IS A `Whose custody`
GIVETH A `Care or custody`
DECIDE `the kind of death` IS
    BRANCH
        IF     `where` EQUALS `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Mental Health Act 2015`
            OR `where` EQUALS `being taken into or detained in custody, or subject to an order, under the Crimes Act 1900 section 309 (assessment whether emergency detention is required)`
        THEN `a death in care (s 3BB)`
        IF     `where` EQUALS `at a correction centre, lockup or detention place`
            OR `where` EQUALS `performing community service work under a good behaviour order (Crimes (Sentencing) Act 2005)`
            OR `where` EQUALS `subject to an accommodation order for a young offender (Crimes (Sentencing) Act 2005 section 133Z)`
            OR `where` EQUALS `subject to an interstate transfer arrangement, or in transit custody, under the Children and Young People Act 2008`
            OR `where` EQUALS `at a licensed place under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994`
        THEN `a death in custody (s 3C)`
        IF     `where` EQUALS `in, being taken into, or after being taken into, someone's custody`
           AND `is a custodial officer` `who`
        THEN `a death in custody (s 3C)`
        IF     `where` EQUALS `escaping, or attempting to escape, from someone's custody`
           AND `is a custodial officer` `who`
           AND (NOT (`who` EQUALS `a carer under the Intoxicated People (Care and Protection) Act 1994`))
        THEN `a death in custody (s 3C)`
        OTHERWISE `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`

@export Was the death a death in care or a death in custody under the Coroners Act (which brings in extra protections for the family)?
GIVEN `where they were` IS A `Where the person was` @desc Where the person was, or what they were subject to, when they died or when they sustained the injury that killed them. Choose none of these if no listed place or circumstance applies
      `whose custody` IS A `Whose custody` @desc If they were in, or escaping from, someone's custody: whose. Otherwise choose no one
GIVETH A `Care or custody`
DECIDE `Was it a death in care or custody question` IS `the kind of death` `where they were` `whose custody`

§§ `s 13 -- must there be an inquest`

-- s 13(1): "A coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death of a person who-- (a) dies violently, or
-- unnaturally, in unknown circumstances; or (b) dies under suspicious circumstances; or (c) dies and the death appears
-- to be completely or partly attributable to an operation or procedure; or (d) dies after having undergone an operation
-- or procedure and in circumstances that, in the opinion of the Chief Coroner, should be better ascertained; or (e) dies
-- and no cause of death certificate has been given by a doctor or nurse practitioner; or (f) dies not having been
-- attended by a doctor or nurse practitioner at any time within the 6 months before the death; or (g) dies after an
-- accident where the cause of death appears to be directly attributable to the accident; or (h) dies, or is suspected to
-- have died, in circumstances that, in the opinion of the Attorney-General, should be better ascertained; or (i) dies in
-- care or custody." (2): "However, subsection (1) (i) does not apply in relation to a person who has self-administered,
-- or been administered, an approved substance in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2024." (3): "A coroner
-- has jurisdiction to hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death, outside the ACT, of a person, if-- (a) the
-- person was ordinarily resident in the ACT; and (b) the death happened in any of the circumstances referred to in
-- subsection (1)." (4): jurisdiction even though the body is outside the ACT, destroyed or unrecoverable, or (suspected
-- death) cannot be found. (5): "operation or procedure ... does not include the administration of an approved substance
-- ... in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2024."
-- READING (finding 1): s 13(1) has no words of place; it is read as applying to deaths in the ACT, since s 13(3) gives
-- only jurisdiction, not a duty, for deaths outside the ACT.
DECLARE `Inquest answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
    `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest, and the death is a death in care or custody, so Part 6 also applies (s 13(1)(i))`
    `a coroner may hold an inquest but is not required to: the death happened outside the ACT to a person ordinarily resident in the ACT, in circumstances s 13(1) lists (s 13(3))`
    `no: s 13(1)(i) does not apply to a person who self-administered, or was administered, an approved substance under the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2024, and no other paragraph of s 13(1) applies (s 13(2))`
    `no: the death happened outside the ACT to a person not ordinarily resident in the ACT, so s 13(3) gives no jurisdiction`
    `not covered: s 13 does not require an inquest on these facts; its list is not closed, and s 12 keeps the coroner's other jurisdiction`

@export Must a coroner hold an inquest into the death?
GIVEN `the death happened in the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died (or is suspected to have died) in the ACT
      `they were ordinarily resident in the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died ordinarily lived in the ACT
      `they died violently or unnaturally in unknown circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died violently, or unnaturally, in unknown circumstances (s 13(1)(a))
      `they died under suspicious circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died under suspicious circumstances (s 13(1)(b))
      `the death appears attributable to an operation or procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death appears to be completely or partly attributable to a medical, surgical, dental or similar operation or an invasive medical or diagnostic procedure (not the administration of a voluntary assisted dying substance) (s 13(1)(c))
      `the Chief Coroner thinks the circumstances after an operation should be better ascertained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died after an operation or procedure in circumstances that, in the Chief Coroner's opinion, should be better ascertained (s 13(1)(d))
      `no cause of death certificate has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether no cause of death certificate has been given by a doctor or nurse practitioner (s 13(1)(e))
      `no doctor or nurse practitioner attended them in the 6 months before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had not been attended by a doctor or nurse practitioner at any time in the 6 months before they died (s 13(1)(f))
      `the death appears directly attributable to an accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died after an accident and the cause of death appears to be directly attributable to it (s 13(1)(g))
      `the Attorney-General thinks the circumstances should be better ascertained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney-General is of the opinion that the circumstances of the death should be better ascertained (s 13(1)(h))
      `where they were` IS A `Where the person was` @desc Where the person was, or what they were subject to, when they died or sustained the fatal injury (for death in care or custody, s 13(1)(i)); choose none of these if none applies
      `whose custody` IS A `Whose custody` @desc If they were in, or escaping from, someone's custody: whose. Otherwise choose no one
      `it was a voluntary assisted death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person self-administered, or was administered, an approved substance in accordance with the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2024
GIVETH AN `Inquest answer`
DECIDE `Must there be an inquest question` IS
    IF (NOT `the death happened in the ACT`)
    THEN IF `they were ordinarily resident in the ACT` AND (`any other paragraph applies` OR `paragraph (i) applies`)
         THEN `a coroner may hold an inquest but is not required to: the death happened outside the ACT to a person ordinarily resident in the ACT, in circumstances s 13(1) lists (s 13(3))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `they were ordinarily resident in the ACT`)
              THEN `no: the death happened outside the ACT to a person not ordinarily resident in the ACT, so s 13(3) gives no jurisdiction`
              ELSE `not covered: s 13 does not require an inquest on these facts; its list is not closed, and s 12 keeps the coroner's other jurisdiction`
    ELSE BRANCH
             IF `paragraph (i) applies`
             THEN `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest, and the death is a death in care or custody, so Part 6 also applies (s 13(1)(i))`
             IF `any other paragraph applies`
             THEN `yes: a coroner must hold an inquest into the manner and cause of death (s 13(1))`
             IF `it was a voluntary assisted death` AND `in care or custody`
             THEN `no: s 13(1)(i) does not apply to a person who self-administered, or was administered, an approved substance under the Voluntary Assisted Dying Act 2024, and no other paragraph of s 13(1) applies (s 13(2))`
             OTHERWISE `not covered: s 13 does not require an inquest on these facts; its list is not closed, and s 12 keeps the coroner's other jurisdiction`
    WHERE
        `in care or custody` MEANS
            NOT (`the kind of death` `where they were` `whose custody` EQUALS `neither a death in care nor a death in custody (ss 3BB, 3C)`)
        `paragraph (i) applies` MEANS `in care or custody` AND (NOT `it was a voluntary assisted death`)
        `any other paragraph applies` MEANS
                `they died violently or unnaturally in unknown circumstances`
            OR  `they died under suspicious circumstances`
            OR  `the death appears attributable to an operation or procedure`
            OR  `the Chief Coroner thinks the circumstances after an operation should be better ascertained`
            OR  `no cause of death certificate has been given`
            OR  `no doctor or nurse practitioner attended them in the 6 months before the death`
            OR  `the death appears directly attributable to an accident`
            OR  `the Attorney-General thinks the circumstances should be better ascertained`

§§ `s 68A -- will the family be told that an inquest will be held`

-- s 68A(1): "A coroner required to hold an inquest must tell a member of the immediate family of a deceased person (a
-- family representative) to whom the inquest relates that an inquest will be held." (2): after the inquest has begun,
-- reasonable steps to give a family representative "any information prescribed by regulation" (no regulation is
-- deposited). (3): the requirement does not apply if the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that no member of the
-- immediate family wishes to be told, or that it is impracticable to tell any of them. (4): the coroner must, as far as
-- practicable, select a family representative in a position to pass information on, and may select more than one.
DECLARE `Told of the inquest answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must tell a member of the immediate family that an inquest will be held; the coroner chooses who (one able to pass the information on), so it may not be me (s 68A(1), (4))`
    `no: the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that no member of the immediate family wishes to be told, or that it is impracticable to tell any of them (s 68A(3)(a))`
    `no: s 68A applies only where a coroner is required to hold an inquest`

@export Will the family be told that an inquest will be held into the death?
GIVEN `an inquest is required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner is required to hold an inquest into the death (see the section 13 question)
      `the coroner is satisfied no family member wishes to be told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that no member of the immediate family wishes to be told
      `the coroner is satisfied it is impracticable to tell any family member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that it is impracticable to tell any member of the immediate family
GIVETH A `Told of the inquest answer`
DECIDE `Will the family be told of the inquest question` IS
    IF (NOT `an inquest is required`)
    THEN `no: s 68A applies only where a coroner is required to hold an inquest`
    ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied no family member wishes to be told` OR `the coroner is satisfied it is impracticable to tell any family member`
         THEN `no: the coroner is satisfied on reasonable grounds that no member of the immediate family wishes to be told, or that it is impracticable to tell any of them (s 68A(3)(a))`
         ELSE `yes: the coroner must tell a member of the immediate family that an inquest will be held; the coroner chooses who (one able to pass the information on), so it may not be me (s 68A(1), (4))`

§§ `s 15 -- release of the body`

-- s 15(1): "This section applies if-- (a) a death happens in relation to which a coroner is required to hold an inquest;
-- and (b) the body of the deceased is in the ACT." (2): "A coroner has control of the body of the deceased until a
-- certificate authorising its release is given under subsection (3)." (3): "A coroner may give a certificate authorising
-- the release of the body of the deceased if satisfied there is no reason why the body should not be buried, cremated,
-- or taken out of the ACT for burial or cremation."
DECLARE `Release answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner has given a certificate authorising the release of the body (s 15(3))`
    `not yet, but the coroner may now give a release certificate, being satisfied there is no reason the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for that (s 15(3)); until then the coroner controls the body (s 15(2))`
    `not yet: the coroner controls the body until a release certificate is given, and may give one only when satisfied there is no reason the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for that (s 15(2), (3))`
    `s 15 does not give the coroner control of the body: it applies only where an inquest is required and the body is in the ACT (s 15(1))`

@export Can the body be released to us for burial or cremation?
GIVEN `an inquest is required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner is required to hold an inquest into the death (see the section 13 question)
      `the body is in the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in the ACT
      `a release certificate has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has given a certificate authorising the release of the body
      `the coroner is satisfied there is no reason not to release` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied there is no reason why the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for burial or cremation
GIVETH A `Release answer`
DECIDE `Can the body be released question` IS
    IF (NOT (`an inquest is required` AND `the body is in the ACT`))
    THEN `s 15 does not give the coroner control of the body: it applies only where an inquest is required and the body is in the ACT (s 15(1))`
    ELSE IF `a release certificate has been given`
         THEN `yes: the coroner has given a certificate authorising the release of the body (s 15(3))`
         ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied there is no reason not to release`
              THEN `not yet, but the coroner may now give a release certificate, being satisfied there is no reason the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for that (s 15(3)); until then the coroner controls the body (s 15(2))`
              ELSE `not yet: the coroner controls the body until a release certificate is given, and may give one only when satisfied there is no reason the body should not be buried, cremated or taken out of the ACT for that (s 15(2), (3))`

§§ `s 20 -- can I ask that there be no post-mortem examination`

-- s 20(1): "A coroner may dispense with a post-mortem examination of a body if the coroner, after considering any medical
-- records or other information given to the coroner relating to the death, is satisfied that the manner and cause of
-- death are sufficiently disclosed." (2): "A coroner may dispense with a post-mortem examination of a body if, on the
-- request of a member of the immediate family of the deceased or a representative of that person, the coroner is
-- satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed."
DECLARE `No post-mortem answer` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: on my request the coroner may dispense with the post-mortem examination, being satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed (s 20(2)); it is a discretion`
    `no: the coroner may dispense with the post-mortem examination only if satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed (s 20)`
    `s 20(2) gives the request only to a member of the immediate family or their representative; the coroner may still dispense with the examination on the information before it (s 20(1))`

@export Can I ask the coroner not to have a post-mortem examination done?
GIVEN `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a member of the deceased's immediate family (see the first question), or the representative of one
      `the coroner is satisfied the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed
GIVETH A `No post-mortem answer`
DECIDE `Can I ask that there be no post-mortem question` IS
    IF (NOT `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative`)
    THEN `s 20(2) gives the request only to a member of the immediate family or their representative; the coroner may still dispense with the examination on the information before it (s 20(1))`
    ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed`
         THEN `possibly: on my request the coroner may dispense with the post-mortem examination, being satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed (s 20(2)); it is a discretion`
         ELSE `no: the coroner may dispense with the post-mortem examination only if satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed (s 20)`

§§ `ss 23, 24, 70 -- viewing the body, inspecting the scene, attending the post-mortem`

-- s 23(1): "A coroner holding an inquest into a death (other than a death in care or death in custody) may, if requested
-- to do so by a member of the immediate family of the deceased or a representative of that person, authorise-- (a) the
-- viewing of the body ...; or (b) an inspection of the scene of the death ...; or (c) an inspection of the scene of an
-- event which, in the opinion of the coroner, may have resulted in the death of the person; or (d) the member or a
-- representative of that member to be present at any post-mortem examination ...; or (e) the same or another doctor to
-- conduct a further post-mortem examination on the body." (2): if the coroner refuses "on the ground that the coroner
-- believes that it would not be in the public interest or the interests of justice to do so", written notice of the
-- refusal and an explanation; (3): the notice must say the person may apply to the Chief Coroner for reconsideration.
-- s 70(1): "The coroner holding an inquest into a death in care or death in custody must, if requested to do so by a
-- member of the immediate family ... authorise-- (a) the viewing of the body ...; or (b) an inspection of the scene of
-- the death ...; or (c) the member or a representative ... to be present at any post-mortem examination ...; or (d) the
-- same or another doctor to conduct a further post-mortem examination on the body; unless the coroner believes, on
-- reasonable grounds, that it would not be in the interests of justice to do so." (2): if not, written notice of the
-- decision and reasons to the person who asked and, for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander deceased, to an
-- appropriate local Aboriginal legal service.
-- s 70 has no counterpart to s 23(1)(c) (finding 3).
DECLARE `What I asked for` IS ONE OF
    `to view the body`
    `to inspect the scene of the death`
    `to inspect the scene of an event that may have resulted in the death`
    `to be present at the post-mortem examination`
    `for the same or another doctor to conduct a further post-mortem examination`

DECLARE `Request answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must authorise it (s 70(1))`
    `no, but the coroner believes on reasonable grounds it would not be in the interests of justice, and must give me written notice of the decision and the reasons (s 70(1), (2))`
    `possibly: the coroner may authorise it; it is a discretion (s 23(1))`
    `possibly, but if the coroner refuses because it would not be in the public interest or the interests of justice, I must be given written notice with an explanation and a statement that I may apply to the Chief Coroner to have the decision reconsidered (s 23(2), (3), s 24)`
    `not covered: for a death in care or custody, s 23 does not apply and s 70 does not list an inspection of the scene of another event (ss 23(1), 70(1))`
    `no: only a member of the immediate family or their representative can make the request (ss 23(1), 70(1))`

@export If I ask to view the body, inspect the scene, attend the post-mortem or have a further post-mortem done, must the coroner allow it?
GIVEN `what I asked for` IS A `What I asked for` @desc What you asked the coroner to authorise. One of: to view the body; to inspect the scene of the death; to inspect the scene of an event that may have resulted in the death; to be present at the post-mortem examination; for the same or another doctor to conduct a further post-mortem examination
      `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a member of the deceased's immediate family, or the representative of one
      `it is a death in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is a death in care or a death in custody (see that question)
      `the coroner believes it would not be in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner believes, on reasonable grounds, that allowing it would not be in the interests of justice
      `the coroner believes it would not be in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner believes that allowing it would not be in the public interest
GIVETH A `Request answer`
DECIDE `Must the coroner allow my request question` IS
    IF (NOT `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative`)
    THEN `no: only a member of the immediate family or their representative can make the request (ss 23(1), 70(1))`
    ELSE IF `it is a death in care or custody`
         THEN IF `what I asked for` EQUALS `to inspect the scene of an event that may have resulted in the death`
              THEN `not covered: for a death in care or custody, s 23 does not apply and s 70 does not list an inspection of the scene of another event (ss 23(1), 70(1))`
              ELSE IF `the coroner believes it would not be in the interests of justice`
                   THEN `no, but the coroner believes on reasonable grounds it would not be in the interests of justice, and must give me written notice of the decision and the reasons (s 70(1), (2))`
                   ELSE `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must authorise it (s 70(1))`
         ELSE IF `the coroner believes it would not be in the interests of justice` OR `the coroner believes it would not be in the public interest`
              THEN `possibly, but if the coroner refuses because it would not be in the public interest or the interests of justice, I must be given written notice with an explanation and a statement that I may apply to the Chief Coroner to have the decision reconsidered (s 23(2), (3), s 24)`
              ELSE `possibly: the coroner may authorise it; it is a discretion (s 23(1))`

-- s 24(1): "Where-- (a) a coroner refuses to give an authorisation under section 23 (1); and (b) the Chief Coroner
-- receives a written application requesting that the coroner who refused to give the authorisation be requested to
-- reconsider the decision; the Chief Coroner must, if satisfied that the applicant has sufficient interest in the
-- decision, request the coroner to whom the application relates to reconsider the decision." (2): "A request must set out
-- the grounds on which the person relies." (3): "The Chief Coroner must notify the applicant of any comments of a coroner
-- in response to the request."
DECLARE `Reconsideration of a refusal answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Chief Coroner must ask the coroner to reconsider, and must tell me of any comments the coroner makes in response (s 24(1), (3))`
    `not yet: the application to the Chief Coroner must be in writing and set out the grounds I rely on (s 24(1)(b), (2))`
    `no: the Chief Coroner must ask for reconsideration only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the decision (s 24(1))`
    `no: s 24 applies only to a refusal under s 23(1), which does not cover a death in care or custody (s 70(2) gives written reasons instead)`

@export The coroner refused my request to view the body, inspect the scene or attend the post-mortem. Can I have the refusal reconsidered?
GIVEN `the refusal was under section 23` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner refused an authorisation under section 23(1), that is, for a death that is not a death in care or custody
      `I applied in writing to the Chief Coroner with my grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied in writing to the Chief Coroner asking for the decision to be reconsidered, setting out your grounds
      `the Chief Coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Coroner is satisfied that you have a sufficient interest in the decision
GIVETH A `Reconsideration of a refusal answer`
DECIDE `Can I have a refusal reconsidered question` IS
    IF (NOT `the refusal was under section 23`)
    THEN `no: s 24 applies only to a refusal under s 23(1), which does not cover a death in care or custody (s 70(2) gives written reasons instead)`
    ELSE IF (NOT `I applied in writing to the Chief Coroner with my grounds`)
         THEN `not yet: the application to the Chief Coroner must be in writing and set out the grounds I rely on (s 24(1)(b), (2))`
         ELSE IF `the Chief Coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest`
              THEN `yes: the Chief Coroner must ask the coroner to reconsider, and must tell me of any comments the coroner makes in response (s 24(1), (3))`
              ELSE `no: the Chief Coroner must ask for reconsideration only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the decision (s 24(1))`

§§ `ss 34A, 37, 38, 69 -- will there be a hearing, and will I be told`

-- s 34A(1): "A coroner may decide not to conduct a hearing into a death if, after consideration of information given to a
-- coroner relating to the death of a person, the coroner is satisfied that-- (a) the manner and cause of death are
-- sufficiently disclosed; and (b) a hearing is unnecessary." (2): "A coroner must not dispense with a hearing into a
-- death of a person, if the coroner has reasonable grounds for believing the death is a death in care or death in
-- custody." (3): written notice of a decision not to hold a hearing, with the grounds, to the Chief Coroner and a member
-- of the immediate family.
DECLARE `Hearing answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must not dispense with a hearing, having reasonable grounds to believe the death is a death in care or custody (s 34A(2))`
    `not necessarily: the coroner may decide not to hold a hearing; if so, the Chief Coroner and a member of the immediate family must get written notice of the decision and its grounds (s 34A(1), (3)), and I may apply for a hearing or for the decision to be reconsidered (s 64)`
    `the coroner cannot decide against a hearing under s 34A unless satisfied both that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed and that a hearing is unnecessary (s 34A(1))`

@export Must there be a public hearing into the death?
GIVEN `the coroner believes it is a death in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has reasonable grounds for believing the death is a death in care or a death in custody
      `the coroner is satisfied the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed
      `the coroner is satisfied a hearing is unnecessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied that a hearing is unnecessary
GIVETH A `Hearing answer`
DECIDE `Must there be a hearing question` IS
    IF `the coroner believes it is a death in care or custody`
    THEN `yes: the coroner must not dispense with a hearing, having reasonable grounds to believe the death is a death in care or custody (s 34A(2))`
    ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed` AND `the coroner is satisfied a hearing is unnecessary`
         THEN `not necessarily: the coroner may decide not to hold a hearing; if so, the Chief Coroner and a member of the immediate family must get written notice of the decision and its grounds (s 34A(1), (3)), and I may apply for a hearing or for the decision to be reconsidered (s 64)`
         ELSE `the coroner cannot decide against a hearing under s 34A unless satisfied both that the manner and cause of death are sufficiently disclosed and that a hearing is unnecessary (s 34A(1))`

-- s 37(1): "Before conducting a hearing for an inquest into a death (other than a death in care or death in custody),
-- the coroner must, at the earliest opportunity, take reasonable steps to notify a member of the immediate family of the
-- deceased person about the time and place of the hearing." (2): "Nothing in subsection (1) prevents a coroner from
-- conducting a hearing if the coroner believes, on reasonable grounds, that it would be in the public interest or the
-- interests of justice to do so."
-- s 69(1): "The coroner must not conduct a hearing into a death in care or death in custody unless satisfied that-- (a)
-- a member of the immediate family of the deceased has been notified of the time and place of the hearing; or (b)
-- reasonable efforts to notify a member of the immediate family of the deceased have been made but were unsuccessful;
-- and, if the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person, the appropriate local Aboriginal legal service
-- has been notified." (2): as s 37(2).
DECLARE `Hearing without the family answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the family has been notified as the Act requires (s 69(1))`
    `yes, but only because the coroner believes on reasonable grounds that it is in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 69(2))`
    `no: the coroner must not hold the hearing until satisfied a member of the immediate family has been notified, or reasonable efforts to notify one have failed, and, for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person, the local Aboriginal legal service has been notified (s 69(1))`
    `yes: the coroner has notified a member of the immediate family, or taken reasonable steps to (s 37(1))`
    `yes, the coroner may go ahead, believing on reasonable grounds that it is in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 37(2))`
    `not yet: before the hearing the coroner must take reasonable steps, at the earliest opportunity, to notify a member of the immediate family of its time and place (s 37(1))`

@export Can the coroner hold the hearing without the family having been told of it?
GIVEN `it is a death in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is a death in care or a death in custody
      `a family member has been notified of the time and place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a member of the immediate family has been notified of the hearing's time and place
      `reasonable efforts to notify a family member were made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner made reasonable efforts (or took reasonable steps) to notify a member of the immediate family, even if they did not succeed
      `the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person
      `the local Aboriginal legal service has been notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the appropriate local Aboriginal legal service has been notified of the hearing
      `the coroner believes going ahead is in the public interest or the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner believes, on reasonable grounds, that holding the hearing anyway is in the public interest or the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Hearing without the family answer`
DECIDE `Can the hearing go ahead without the family being told question` IS
    IF `it is a death in care or custody`
    THEN IF     (`a family member has been notified of the time and place` OR `reasonable efforts to notify a family member were made`)
            AND ((NOT `the deceased was an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person`) OR `the local Aboriginal legal service has been notified`)
         THEN `yes: the family has been notified as the Act requires (s 69(1))`
         ELSE IF `the coroner believes going ahead is in the public interest or the interests of justice`
              THEN `yes, but only because the coroner believes on reasonable grounds that it is in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 69(2))`
              ELSE `no: the coroner must not hold the hearing until satisfied a member of the immediate family has been notified, or reasonable efforts to notify one have failed, and, for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person, the local Aboriginal legal service has been notified (s 69(1))`
    ELSE IF `a family member has been notified of the time and place` OR `reasonable efforts to notify a family member were made`
         THEN `yes: the coroner has notified a member of the immediate family, or taken reasonable steps to (s 37(1))`
         ELSE IF `the coroner believes going ahead is in the public interest or the interests of justice`
              THEN `yes, the coroner may go ahead, believing on reasonable grounds that it is in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 37(2))`
              ELSE `not yet: before the hearing the coroner must take reasonable steps, at the earliest opportunity, to notify a member of the immediate family of its time and place (s 37(1))`

-- s 38: "A coroner must, where practicable, not less than 28 days before conducting a hearing, give public notice of the
-- date, time and place of the hearing." READING: notice given on the day 28 days before the hearing is in time.
@export By what day should the coroner give public notice of the hearing?
GIVEN `the hearing date` IS A DATE @desc The date the hearing is to start (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-10)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day for public notice of the hearing question` IS 28 `days before` `the hearing date`

DECLARE `Public notice answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: public notice was given at least 28 days before the hearing (s 38)`
    `late: public notice was given less than 28 days before the hearing, and it was practicable to give it in time (s 38)`
    `s 38 asks for 28 days' notice only where practicable; the coroner considered it was not practicable`

@export Was public notice of the hearing given in time?
GIVEN `the hearing date` IS A DATE @desc The date the hearing is to start (ISO 8601)
      `the public notice date` IS A DATE @desc The date public notice of the hearing was given on an ACT government website or in a daily newspaper (ISO 8601)
      `it was practicable to give 28 days notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was practicable to give public notice at least 28 days before the hearing
GIVETH A `Public notice answer`
DECIDE `Was public notice in time question` IS
    IF `the public notice date` `is on or before` (28 `days before` `the hearing date`)
    THEN `in time: public notice was given at least 28 days before the hearing (s 38)`
    ELSE IF `it was practicable to give 28 days notice`
         THEN `late: public notice was given less than 28 days before the hearing, and it was practicable to give it in time (s 38)`
         ELSE `s 38 asks for 28 days' notice only where practicable; the coroner considered it was not practicable`

§§ `ss 42, 49, 57A -- taking part, the record, and correcting the findings`

-- s 42: "A coroner may grant leave to a person-- (a) who has been summoned to give evidence in relation to an inquest or
-- inquiry; or (b) who, in the opinion of the coroner, has a sufficient interest in the subject matter of the inquest or
-- inquiry; to appear in person at a hearing or to be represented by a lawyer and, at the hearing, to examine and
-- cross-examine witnesses on matters relevant to the inquest or inquiry to which the hearing relates."
DECLARE `Taking part answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner has given me leave to appear in person or by a lawyer and to examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42)`
    `I may ask for leave: the coroner may let me appear in person or by a lawyer and examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42); it is a discretion`
    `not covered: s 42 leave is for a person summoned to give evidence or whom the coroner considers to have a sufficient interest; the hearing itself is in public unless the coroner orders otherwise (s 40)`

@export Can I take part in the hearing, myself or through a lawyer, and question witnesses?
GIVEN `I have been summoned to give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been summoned to give evidence at the inquest
      `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest in the subject matter of the inquest (a close family member usually will, but it is the coroner's opinion)
      `the coroner has granted me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has already granted you leave to appear
GIVETH A `Taking part answer`
DECIDE `Can I take part in the hearing question` IS
    IF (NOT (`I have been summoned to give evidence` OR `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest`))
    THEN `not covered: s 42 leave is for a person summoned to give evidence or whom the coroner considers to have a sufficient interest; the hearing itself is in public unless the coroner orders otherwise (s 40)`
    ELSE IF `the coroner has granted me leave`
         THEN `yes: the coroner has given me leave to appear in person or by a lawyer and to examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42)`
         ELSE `I may ask for leave: the coroner may let me appear in person or by a lawyer and examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 42); it is a discretion`

-- s 49(2)(c): the Magistrates Court Act 1930 s 315 applies with this subsection substituted: "(2) A person is entitled to
-- a copy of a record made at an inquest or inquiry only if the person has satisfied the registrar or a coroner that the
-- person has good reason for applying for the copy."
DECLARE `Record answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: having satisfied the registrar or a coroner that I have good reason, I am entitled to a copy of the record (s 49(2)(c))`
    `no: I am entitled to a copy of the record only if I satisfy the registrar or a coroner that I have good reason (s 49(2)(c))`

@export Can I get a copy of the record of the evidence given at the inquest?
GIVEN `I have satisfied the registrar or coroner I have good reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have satisfied the registrar or a coroner that you have good reason for applying for a copy of the record
GIVETH A `Record answer`
DECIDE `Can I get a copy of the record question` IS
    IF `I have satisfied the registrar or coroner I have good reason`
    THEN `yes: having satisfied the registrar or a coroner that I have good reason, I am entitled to a copy of the record (s 49(2)(c))`
    ELSE `no: I am entitled to a copy of the record only if I satisfy the registrar or a coroner that I have good reason (s 49(2)(c))`

-- s 57A(1): "A coroner may amend a finding or report in relation to an inquest or inquiry to correct a mistake, error or
-- omission in the finding or report." (2): "The amendment may be made-- (a) on the coroner's own initiative; or (b) on
-- request by a person with sufficient interest in the inquest or inquiry."
DECLARE `Correction answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, I may ask: the coroner may amend the finding or report to correct a mistake, error or omission, on my request as a person with sufficient interest (s 57A); it is a discretion`
    `s 57A lets a person with sufficient interest ask; the coroner may still correct it on his or her own initiative (s 57A(2)(a))`
    `no: s 57A is for correcting a mistake, error or omission, not for changing a finding I disagree with (see ss 64, 68, 93)`

@export Can I ask the coroner to correct a mistake in the findings?
GIVEN `the finding has a mistake, error or omission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you want changed is a mistake, error or omission in the finding or report (rather than a finding you disagree with)
      `I have a sufficient interest in the inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a sufficient interest in the inquest (a member of the immediate family usually will)
GIVETH A `Correction answer`
DECIDE `Can a mistake in the findings be corrected question` IS
    IF (NOT `the finding has a mistake, error or omission`)
    THEN `no: s 57A is for correcting a mistake, error or omission, not for changing a finding I disagree with (see ss 64, 68, 93)`
    ELSE IF `I have a sufficient interest in the inquest`
         THEN `yes, I may ask: the coroner may amend the finding or report to correct a mistake, error or omission, on my request as a person with sufficient interest (s 57A); it is a discretion`
         ELSE `s 57A lets a person with sufficient interest ask; the coroner may still correct it on his or her own initiative (s 57A(2)(a))`

§§ `ss 52, 54, 74, 75, 76 -- the findings`

-- s 52(1): the coroner "must find, if possible-- (a) the identity of the deceased; and (b) when and where the death
-- happened; and (c) the manner and cause of death; and (d) in the case of the suspected death of a person--that the
-- person has died." (4)(a): must state whether a matter of public safety is found to arise and, if so, comment on it.
-- s 74: for a death in care or custody the coroner "must include in a record of the proceedings of the inquest findings
-- about the quality of care, treatment and supervision of the deceased that, in the opinion of the coroner, contributed
-- to the cause of death."
DECLARE `What the findings cover` IS ONE OF
    `the identity of the deceased, when and where the death happened, and the manner and cause of death, so far as possible; and whether a matter of public safety arises, with comment if it does (s 52(1), (4))`
    `those, and also findings about the quality of care, treatment and supervision of the deceased that the coroner thinks contributed to the cause of death (ss 52, 74)`

@export What must the coroner's findings cover?
GIVEN `it is a death in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is a death in care or a death in custody
GIVETH A `What the findings cover`
DECIDE `What must the findings cover question` IS
    IF `it is a death in care or custody`
    THEN `those, and also findings about the quality of care, treatment and supervision of the deceased that the coroner thinks contributed to the cause of death (ss 52, 74)`
    ELSE `the identity of the deceased, when and where the death happened, and the manner and cause of death, so far as possible; and whether a matter of public safety arises, with comment if it does (s 52(1), (4))`

-- s 54(1): "A coroner holding an inquest (other than an inquest into a death in care or death in custody) must, on the
-- request of a member of the immediate family of the deceased or a representative of that member, make available a copy
-- of the coroner's findings to that member or representative."
-- s 75(2): "The coroner must make available a copy of a report of the findings into a death in care or death in custody
-- to-- (a) a member of the immediate family of the deceased or a representative of that member; and (b) a witness who
-- appeared at an inquest into the death." s 76(4): the coroner must give a copy of the agency's response "to each person
-- or agency to whom a copy of the report was given under section 75."
DECLARE `Findings copy answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must make a copy of the report of the findings available to me without my asking, and later a copy of the agency's response (ss 75(2), 76(4))`
    `yes: I have asked, and the coroner must make a copy of the findings available to me (s 54(1))`
    `yes, if I ask: on my request the coroner must make a copy of the findings available to me (s 54(1))`
    `not covered: ss 54 and 75 give a copy only to a member of the immediate family or their representative (and, for a death in care or custody, a witness who appeared)`

@export Can I get a copy of the coroner's findings?
GIVEN `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a member of the deceased's immediate family, or the representative of one
      `I appeared as a witness at the inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you appeared as a witness at the inquest
      `it is a death in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is a death in care or a death in custody
      `I have asked for a copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the coroner for a copy of the findings
GIVETH A `Findings copy answer`
DECIDE `Can I get a copy of the findings question` IS
    IF `it is a death in care or custody`
    THEN IF `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative` OR `I appeared as a witness at the inquest`
         THEN `yes: for a death in care or custody the coroner must make a copy of the report of the findings available to me without my asking, and later a copy of the agency's response (ss 75(2), 76(4))`
         ELSE `not covered: ss 54 and 75 give a copy only to a member of the immediate family or their representative (and, for a death in care or custody, a witness who appeared)`
    ELSE IF `I am a member of the immediate family or their representative`
         THEN IF `I have asked for a copy`
              THEN `yes: I have asked, and the coroner must make a copy of the findings available to me (s 54(1))`
              ELSE `yes, if I ask: on my request the coroner must make a copy of the findings available to me (s 54(1))`
         ELSE `not covered: ss 54 and 75 give a copy only to a member of the immediate family or their representative (and, for a death in care or custody, a witness who appeared)`

-- s 76(1): "The agency to which a report is given under section 75 must, not later than 3 months after the date of receipt
-- of the report, give to the Minister responsible for the agency a written response to the findings contained in the
-- report." The response then goes to the coroner (s 76(3)) and on to the family (s 76(4)); s 76(3) sets no fixed day.
@export For a death in care or custody: by what day must the agency the person was in the care or custody of respond to the coroner's findings?
GIVEN `the day the agency received the report` IS A DATE @desc The date the agency the person was in the care or custody of received the coroner's report of the findings (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day for the agency's response question` IS 3 `months after` `the day the agency received the report`

§§ `ss 58, 58A -- the inquest is paused because of a prosecution (only if a prosecution arises)`

-- s 58(3): where the coroner believes on reasonable grounds that a person mentioned at the inquest has committed an
-- indictable offence, the coroner must tell the DPP and, for a related indictable offence, "must not proceed further
-- with the inquest ... until the day worked out under section 58A", other than to establish the death, the person's
-- identity and the date and place of death. (4), (5): the same where the DPP tells the coroner an indictment will be
-- presented, or one is presented.
-- s 58A(1): "the coroner may proceed with the inquest or inquiry-- (a) if a prosecution is not started on or before the
-- day after the day that is 3 months after the day the coroner-- (i) gave notice to the director of public prosecutions
-- under section 58 (3) (a); or (ii) received notice from the director of public prosecutions under section 58 (4) (a);
-- or (b) on a day after-- (i) the day the director of public prosecutions gives notice to the coroner that-- (A) no
-- indictment is to be presented ...; or (B) ... the director ... has discontinued or intends to discontinue the
-- proceeding ...; or (ii) if the person is not committed to stand trial for the offence (the person is discharged), and
-- is not indicted for the offence ... within 28 days after the day the person is discharged--30 days after the day the
-- person is discharged; or (iii) if the person is committed for trial or indicted for the offence--the day after the day
-- the director of public prosecutions gives notice to the coroner that the proceeding for the offence has been finally
-- decided; or (iv) if the person is found guilty of the offence, and the director ... has not given notice under
-- subparagraph (iii) ...--30 days after the proceeding is finally decided." (2): the coroner may then continue, "but must
-- not make a finding inconsistent with the judgment or verdict".
-- READING (finding 5): under (b) the inquest may go on from the day after the day each subparagraph names. So (i) the day
-- after the DPP's notice; (ii) the day after the 30th day after the discharge; (iii) the day after "the day after the day"
-- of the DPP's notice, which is the second day after it; (iv) the day after the 30th day after the final decision. Under
-- (a), the last day on which a prosecution may be started is the day after the day 3 months on, and the inquest may go
-- on from the day after that.
DECLARE `What has happened since the referral` IS ONE OF
    `no prosecution has been started`
    `the DPP has given notice that no indictment will be presented, or that the proceeding has been or will be discontinued`
    `the person was not committed for trial (discharged) and was not indicted within 28 days`
    `the person was committed for trial or indicted, and the DPP has given notice that the proceeding has been finally decided`
    `the person was found guilty, and the DPP has given no notice that the proceeding has been finally decided`
    `a prosecution is under way and none of these has happened`

DECLARE `Resumption answer` IS ONE OF
    `the inquest may go on from` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `not yet: while the prosecution runs, the inquest may only establish the death, the person's identity and the date and place of death (s 58(3)(b), (5))`

@export The inquest was paused because of a possible prosecution. From what day may it go on?
GIVEN `what has happened` IS A `What has happened since the referral` @desc What has happened since the coroner told the DPP, or the DPP told the coroner, about a related indictable offence. One of: no prosecution has been started; the DPP has given notice that no indictment will be presented or the proceeding is discontinued; the person was discharged and not indicted within 28 days; the person was committed or indicted and the DPP has given notice the proceeding is finally decided; the person was found guilty and the DPP has given no such notice; a prosecution is under way and none of these has happened
      `the date that counts` IS A DATE @desc The date the period runs from (ISO 8601): for no prosecution, the day the coroner gave notice to the DPP or received notice from the DPP; for a DPP notice, the day of that notice; for a discharge, the day the person was discharged; for a guilty finding with no DPP notice, the day the proceeding was finally decided
GIVETH A `Resumption answer`
DECIDE `When may the paused inquest go on question` IS
    CONSIDER `what has happened`
    WHEN `no prosecution has been started` THEN `the inquest may go on from` (2 `days after` (3 `months after` `the date that counts`))
    WHEN `the DPP has given notice that no indictment will be presented, or that the proceeding has been or will be discontinued` THEN `the inquest may go on from` (1 `days after` `the date that counts`)
    WHEN `the person was not committed for trial (discharged) and was not indicted within 28 days` THEN `the inquest may go on from` (31 `days after` `the date that counts`)
    WHEN `the person was committed for trial or indicted, and the DPP has given notice that the proceeding has been finally decided` THEN `the inquest may go on from` (2 `days after` `the date that counts`)
    WHEN `the person was found guilty, and the DPP has given no notice that the proceeding has been finally decided` THEN `the inquest may go on from` (31 `days after` `the date that counts`)
    WHEN `a prosecution is under way and none of these has happened` THEN `not yet: while the prosecution runs, the inquest may only establish the death, the person's identity and the date and place of death (s 58(3)(b), (5))`

§§ `ss 64, 90 -- asking for a hearing, or for a decision to be reconsidered (only if there was no hearing, or it was cut short)`

-- s 64(1): "The Chief Coroner may, on application in writing by a person, arrange for a hearing to be conducted for an
-- inquest or an inquiry into a fire." (2): "Subject to subsection (3), the Chief Coroner must-- (a) on application by a
-- person requesting that a coroner who made a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing, reconsider the
-- decision; and (b) if satisfied that the applicant has sufficient interest in the inquest ...; request the coroner to
-- whom the application relates to reconsider the decision." (3): if the decision was the Chief Coroner's own, the Chief
-- Coroner must, if so satisfied, reconsider it. (4): "An application made to the Chief Coroner must-- (a) be in writing;
-- and (b) set out the grounds on which the person relies."
DECLARE `What I want from the Chief Coroner` IS ONE OF
    `a hearing`
    `reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing`

DECLARE `Section 64 answer` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the Chief Coroner may arrange for a hearing on my written application (s 64(1)); it is a discretion`
    `yes: the Chief Coroner must ask the coroner to reconsider; the coroner must respond within 14 days of the request, and within 14 days of that response the Chief Coroner must tell me in writing whether a hearing will be held (s 64(2), (5), (7))`
    `yes: the decision was the Chief Coroner's own, and the Chief Coroner must reconsider it (s 64(3))`
    `no: the Chief Coroner must act on my application only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the inquest (s 64(2)(b), (3))`
    `not yet: my application must be in writing and set out the grounds I rely on (s 64(4))`

@export There was no hearing, or the hearing was cut short. Can I ask the Chief Coroner for a hearing, or for the decision to be reconsidered?
GIVEN `what I want` IS A `What I want from the Chief Coroner` @desc What you are asking the Chief Coroner for. One of: a hearing; reconsideration of a decision to dispense with or to conclude a hearing
      `my application is in writing with my grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application to the Chief Coroner is in writing and sets out the grounds you rely on
      `the Chief Coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Coroner is satisfied that you have a sufficient interest in the inquest
      `the decision was the Chief Coroner's own` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision to dispense with or conclude the hearing was made by the Chief Coroner
GIVETH A `Section 64 answer`
DECIDE `Can I ask for a hearing or a reconsideration question` IS
    IF (NOT `my application is in writing with my grounds`)
    THEN `not yet: my application must be in writing and set out the grounds I rely on (s 64(4))`
    ELSE IF `what I want` EQUALS `a hearing`
         THEN `possibly: the Chief Coroner may arrange for a hearing on my written application (s 64(1)); it is a discretion`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the Chief Coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest`)
              THEN `no: the Chief Coroner must act on my application only if satisfied that I have a sufficient interest in the inquest (s 64(2)(b), (3))`
              ELSE IF `the decision was the Chief Coroner's own`
                   THEN `yes: the decision was the Chief Coroner's own, and the Chief Coroner must reconsider it (s 64(3))`
                   ELSE `yes: the Chief Coroner must ask the coroner to reconsider; the coroner must respond within 14 days of the request, and within 14 days of that response the Chief Coroner must tell me in writing whether a hearing will be held (s 64(2), (5), (7))`

-- The s 64 deadlines as a regulative rule. s 64(5): "The coroner must respond to the Chief Coroner's request within 14
-- days after receipt of the request." (7): "The Chief Coroner, within 14 days after receiving the coroner's response
-- under subsection (5), must-- (a) if the coroner intends to conduct a hearing, or the Chief Coroner is satisfied under
-- subsection (6) that a hearing should be conducted--give the applicant notice in writing that a hearing will be
-- conducted; or (b) if the Chief Coroner is not satisfied ...--give the applicant ... (i) notice in writing that a hearing
-- will not be conducted; ..." s 90: "A person to whom notice under section 64 (7) (b) (i) has been given may, within 30
-- days after receipt of the notice, apply to the Supreme Court for an order that a hearing into a death or fire be
-- conducted."
-- Time is in days from the coroner's receipt of the Chief Coroner's request. READING: the applicant receives the Chief
-- Coroner's notice on the day it is given. s 64(6)(b) (the coroner unavailable, so no response) is not modelled.
DECLARE `Section 64 party` IS ONE OF
    `the coroner who made the decision`
    `the Chief Coroner`
    `I, the applicant`

DECLARE `Section 64 act` IS ONE OF
    `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider`
    `give me written notice that a hearing will be conducted`
    `give me written notice that a hearing will not be conducted, with the statements s 64(7)(b) requires`
    `apply to the Supreme Court for an order that a hearing be conducted`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Section 64 party` `Section 64 act`
DECIDE `s 64(5), (7) and s 90 -- what must happen after my request is passed on` IS
    PARTY `the coroner who made the decision`
    MUST  `respond to the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE
        (PARTY `the Chief Coroner`
         MUST  `give me written notice that a hearing will be conducted`
         WITHIN 14)
        ROR
        (PARTY `the Chief Coroner`
         MUST  `give me written notice that a hearing will not be conducted, with the statements s 64(7)(b) requires`
         WITHIN 14
         HENCE
            (PARTY `I, the applicant`
             MAY   `apply to the Supreme Court for an order that a hearing be conducted`
             WITHIN 30)
         LEST  BREACH BY `the Chief Coroner` BECAUSE "s 64(7): the Chief Coroner did not give me written notice within 14 days after receiving the coroner's response")
    LEST  BREACH BY `the coroner who made the decision` BECAUSE "s 64(5): the coroner did not respond to the Chief Coroner's request within 14 days after receiving it"

@export After the Chief Coroner passes my request on: by what day must the coroner respond?
GIVEN `the day the coroner received the Chief Coroner's request` IS A DATE @desc The date the coroner received the Chief Coroner's request to reconsider (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day for the coroner's response question` IS 14 `days after` `the day the coroner received the Chief Coroner's request`

@export By what day must the Chief Coroner tell me in writing whether a hearing will be held?
GIVEN `the day the Chief Coroner received the coroner's response` IS A DATE @desc The date the Chief Coroner received the coroner's response to the request to reconsider (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day for the Chief Coroner's notice question` IS 14 `days after` `the day the Chief Coroner received the coroner's response`

@export The Chief Coroner told me there will be no hearing. By what day must I apply to the Supreme Court under section 90?
GIVEN `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the Chief Coroner's written notice that a hearing will not be conducted (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day to apply to the Supreme Court question` IS 30 `days after` `the day I received the notice`

§§ `ss 68, 90 to 93 -- a fresh inquest, and the Supreme Court`

-- s 90 (above). s 91: "The Supreme Court may, on application by a person, if it is of the opinion that it would be in
-- the interests of justice to do so, make an order directing a coroner to conduct a hearing into a death or fire."
-- s 92(1): the Supreme Court may order a hearing on an application "by anyone else" if satisfied that "(a) a coroner does
-- not intend to conduct a hearing ...; and (b) it is in the public interest or the interests of justice that a hearing
-- ... should be conducted"; (2): the application must be served on the Attorney-General. Neither s 91 nor s 92 sets a
-- time limit (finding 6).
DECLARE `Supreme Court hearing answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, under s 90: I am within 30 days after receiving the Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing; I may also rely on ss 91 and 92`
    `not under s 90, which allows 30 days after I received the Chief Coroner's notice; but ss 91 and 92 set no time limit, and the Supreme Court may order a hearing if it considers it in the interests of justice (s 91) or in the public interest or the interests of justice where the coroner does not intend to hold one (s 92)`
    `not under s 90, which needs a Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing (s 64(7)(b)(i)); but I may apply under ss 91 and 92, which set no time limit, serving a s 92 application on the Attorney-General`

@export Can I still apply to the Supreme Court for an order that there be a hearing?
GIVEN `I received a Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Coroner has given you written notice under section 64(7)(b)(i) that a hearing will not be conducted
      `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc The date you received that notice (ISO 8601); if there was none, any date
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The date you apply, or will apply, to the Supreme Court (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Supreme Court hearing answer`
DECIDE `Can I apply to the Supreme Court for a hearing question` IS
    IF (NOT `I received a Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing`)
    THEN `not under s 90, which needs a Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing (s 64(7)(b)(i)); but I may apply under ss 91 and 92, which set no time limit, serving a s 92 application on the Attorney-General`
    ELSE IF `the day I apply` `is on or before` (30 `days after` `the day I received the notice`)
         THEN `yes, under s 90: I am within 30 days after receiving the Chief Coroner's notice that there will be no hearing; I may also rely on ss 91 and 92`
         ELSE `not under s 90, which allows 30 days after I received the Chief Coroner's notice; but ss 91 and 92 set no time limit, and the Supreme Court may order a hearing if it considers it in the interests of justice (s 91) or in the public interest or the interests of justice where the coroner does not intend to hold one (s 92)`

-- s 68(1): "Even though an inquest or an inquiry into a fire has been completed, the Chief Coroner may-- (a) of the Chief
-- Coroner's motion; or (b) at the request of a person; arrange for the holding of a fresh inquest or an inquiry into the
-- fire." (2): "The Chief Coroner must not arrange for the holding of an inquest or inquiry under subsection (1) unless
-- satisfied that-- (a) by reason of the discovery of new facts or evidence of material significance to the inquest or
-- inquiry that was not available to be put before a coroner at the time of the previous inquest or inquiry; and (b) it
-- is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice to do so."
-- s 93(1): if an inquest has been held and the Supreme Court, on an application "by anyone else", "is satisfied that,
-- because of fraud, rejection of evidence, irregularity of proceedings, insufficiency of inquiry, discovery of new facts
-- or evidence or otherwise, it is necessary or desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice that the
-- inquest ... be quashed and that another inquest ... be held", it "may order" that. (2): served on the Attorney-General.
DECLARE `Fresh inquest answer` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: at my request the Chief Coroner may arrange a fresh inquest, being satisfied of new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68); and the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another (s 93)`
    `not from the Chief Coroner, who must not arrange a fresh inquest unless satisfied both that there are new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68(2)); but the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another if satisfied it is necessary or desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 93), on an application served on the Attorney-General`
    `no: ss 68 and 93 apply only once the inquest has been completed (held)`

@export The inquest is over. Can there be a fresh inquest?
GIVEN `the inquest has been completed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the inquest has been completed
      `there are new facts or evidence not available before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Coroner is satisfied there are new facts or evidence of material significance that were not available to be put before the coroner at the earlier inquest
      `a fresh inquest is desirable in the public interest or interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Coroner is satisfied that a fresh inquest is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Fresh inquest answer`
DECIDE `Can there be a fresh inquest question` IS
    IF (NOT `the inquest has been completed`)
    THEN `no: ss 68 and 93 apply only once the inquest has been completed (held)`
    ELSE IF `there are new facts or evidence not available before` AND `a fresh inquest is desirable in the public interest or interests of justice`
         THEN `possibly: at my request the Chief Coroner may arrange a fresh inquest, being satisfied of new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68); and the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another (s 93)`
         ELSE `not from the Chief Coroner, who must not arrange a fresh inquest unless satisfied both that there are new facts or evidence of material significance not available before and that it is desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 68(2)); but the Supreme Court may quash the inquest and order another if satisfied it is necessary or desirable in the public interest or the interests of justice (s 93), on an application served on the Attorney-General`

§§ `s 67 -- getting back what the coroner took or kept (only if the coroner kept documents or things)`

-- s 67(1)(b): the coroner may "retain possession of the document or thing for the period necessary for the inquest".
-- (2): "A person otherwise entitled to possession of a document retained under subsection (1) (b) is entitled to be
-- supplied with a copy of the document certified by the coroner to be a true copy." (4): until then the coroner must
-- permit that person to inspect and copy it. (5): "If the retention of a document or other thing by a coroner ceases to
-- be necessary for an inquest or inquiry, the coroner must, if a person who appears to the coroner to be entitled to the
-- document or thing requests, give the document or thing to the person." (6): "On the completion of an inquest or
-- inquiry, a coroner must take all reasonably practical steps to give any thing taken or seized under this division to
-- the person whom the coroner reasonably believes to be entitled to it."
DECLARE `Return answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the inquest is complete, and the coroner must take all reasonably practical steps to give it to the person the coroner reasonably believes is entitled to it (s 67(6))`
    `yes, if I ask: the coroner no longer needs it, and must give it to me on my request (s 67(5))`
    `not yet, but I am entitled to a certified copy of the document, and until I get one the coroner must let me inspect and copy it (s 67(2), (4))`
    `not yet: the coroner may keep it for as long as the inquest needs it (s 67(1)(b))`
    `no: the coroner gives it back to the person who appears to the coroner to be entitled to it (s 67(5), (6))`

@export Can I get back documents or belongings the coroner took or kept?
GIVEN `the coroner considers I am entitled to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you appear to the coroner to be the person entitled to the document or thing (for example as the owner, or as the person entitled to the deceased's belongings)
      `it is a document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a document (rather than another thing)
      `the coroner still needs it for the inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether keeping it is still necessary for the inquest
      `the inquest is complete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the inquest has been completed
GIVETH A `Return answer`
DECIDE `Can I get back what the coroner kept question` IS
    IF (NOT `the coroner considers I am entitled to it`)
    THEN `no: the coroner gives it back to the person who appears to the coroner to be entitled to it (s 67(5), (6))`
    ELSE IF `the inquest is complete`
         THEN `yes: the inquest is complete, and the coroner must take all reasonably practical steps to give it to the person the coroner reasonably believes is entitled to it (s 67(6))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the coroner still needs it for the inquest`)
              THEN `yes, if I ask: the coroner no longer needs it, and must give it to me on my request (s 67(5))`
              ELSE IF `it is a document`
                   THEN `not yet, but I am entitled to a certified copy of the document, and until I get one the coroner must let me inspect and copy it (s 67(2), (4))`
                   ELSE `not yet: the coroner may keep it for as long as the inquest needs it (s 67(1)(b))`

§§ `ss 40, 65, 77, 83 -- my own duties, and the offences`

-- s 77(1): "A person commits an offence if the person-- (a) knows that a death has happened; and (b) has reasonable
-- grounds to believe that-- (i) a coroner would have jurisdiction to hold an inquest in relation to the death; and (ii)
-- the death has not been reported to a coroner or a police officer; and (c) does not report the death to a coroner or a
-- police officer as soon as practicable after becoming aware of it and having the reasonable grounds mentioned in
-- paragraph (b). Maximum penalty: 50 penalty units, imprisonment for 6 months or both."
DECLARE `Reporting answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I must report the death to a coroner or a police officer as soon as practicable; not doing so is an offence, maximum 50 penalty units, 6 months' imprisonment or both (s 77(1))`
    `no: s 77(1) applies only to a person who knows of the death and has reasonable grounds to believe a coroner would have jurisdiction and that it has not been reported`

@export Must I report the death to a coroner or the police?
GIVEN `I know the death has happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you know that the death has happened
      `I believe a coroner would have jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe a coroner would have jurisdiction to hold an inquest into the death (see the section 13 question)
      `I believe it has not been reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe the death has not been reported to a coroner or a police officer
GIVETH A `Reporting answer`
DECIDE `Must I report the death question` IS
    IF `I know the death has happened` AND `I believe a coroner would have jurisdiction` AND `I believe it has not been reported`
    THEN `yes: I must report the death to a coroner or a police officer as soon as practicable; not doing so is an offence, maximum 50 penalty units, 6 months' imprisonment or both (s 77(1))`
    ELSE `no: s 77(1) applies only to a person who knows of the death and has reasonable grounds to believe a coroner would have jurisdiction and that it has not been reported`

-- s 65(1): the coroner "may take reasonable steps to restrict access to the scene of the death of a person, the scene of
-- an event that, in the opinion of the coroner, may have resulted in the death, or the place where the fire or disaster
-- happened." (2): "A person commits an offence if the person enters or interferes with an area to which access is
-- restricted under subsection (1). Maximum penalty: 100 penalty units." (3): strict liability. (4): "This section does
-- not apply to a police officer, a member of an emergency service or a person assisting the coroner unless the coroner
-- orders otherwise."
DECLARE `Restricted area answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted is a strict liability offence, maximum 100 penalty units (s 65(2), (3)); viewing or inspecting it needs the coroner's authorisation (ss 23, 70)`
    `s 65 does not stop me: it does not apply to a police officer, a member of an emergency service or a person assisting the coroner, unless the coroner orders otherwise (s 65(4))`
    `s 65 does not stop me: the coroner has not restricted access to the area`

@export May I go into the scene of the death?
GIVEN `the coroner has restricted access to the area` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has restricted access to the scene of the death, the scene of an event that may have caused it, or the place of a fire or disaster
      `I am a police officer, emergency service member or assisting the coroner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a police officer, a member of an emergency service or a person assisting the coroner
      `the coroner has ordered that the restriction applies to me too` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has ordered that the restriction applies even to police, emergency services or people assisting
GIVETH A `Restricted area answer`
DECIDE `May I enter the scene question` IS
    IF (NOT `the coroner has restricted access to the area`)
    THEN `s 65 does not stop me: the coroner has not restricted access to the area`
    ELSE IF `I am a police officer, emergency service member or assisting the coroner` AND (NOT `the coroner has ordered that the restriction applies to me too`)
         THEN `s 65 does not stop me: it does not apply to a police officer, a member of an emergency service or a person assisting the coroner, unless the coroner orders otherwise (s 65(4))`
         ELSE `no: entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted is a strict liability offence, maximum 100 penalty units (s 65(2), (3)); viewing or inspecting it needs the coroner's authorisation (ss 23, 70)`

-- The penalties. The value of a penalty unit is set by the Legislation Act 2001, s 133, which is not deposited, so the
-- answer is in penalty units.
-- s 40(3): contravening an order for a private hearing or a non-publication direction: "100 penalty units, imprisonment
-- for 1 year or both". s 65(2): 100 penalty units. s 77(1): "50 penalty units, imprisonment for 6 months or both".
-- s 83: interfering with or removing a body or ashes "with the intention of preventing or hindering" a post-mortem or
-- analysis: "200 penalty units, imprisonment for 2 years or both".
DECLARE `Coronial offence` IS ONE OF
    `breaching an order that a hearing be private, or a direction not to publish evidence (s 40(3))`
    `entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted (s 65(2))`
    `not reporting a reportable death (s 77(1))`
    `interfering with or removing a body or ashes to prevent or hinder a post-mortem or analysis (s 83)`

DECLARE `Maximum penalty` HAS
    `penalty units` IS A NUMBER
    `months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER

@export What is the most I can be fined or imprisoned for an offence under the Coroners Act?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Coronial offence` @desc Which offence. One of: breaching a private-hearing order or non-publication direction (s 40(3)); entering or interfering with a restricted area (s 65(2)); not reporting a reportable death (s 77(1)); interfering with or removing a body or ashes to prevent a post-mortem (s 83)
GIVETH A `Maximum penalty`
DECIDE `What is the maximum penalty question` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `breaching an order that a hearing be private, or a direction not to publish evidence (s 40(3))` THEN `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 100, `months of imprisonment` IS 12
    WHEN `entering or interfering with an area the coroner has restricted (s 65(2))` THEN `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 100, `months of imprisonment` IS 0
    WHEN `not reporting a reportable death (s 77(1))` THEN `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 50, `months of imprisonment` IS 6
    WHEN `interfering with or removing a body or ashes to prevent or hinder a post-mortem or analysis (s 83)` THEN `Maximum penalty` WITH `penalty units` IS 200, `months of imprisonment` IS 24
